Chapter: GopWake
  cites Verify chapter WakeCeremony
  cites Verify chapter CdxBinary
  cites Foreword chapter Sha256
  cites Foreword chapter Ed25519

 The wake ceremony over a seed that lives in a raw buffer (B3.4). The
 ceremony itself is `codex/os/verify/WakeCeremony`, and its meaning is
 unchanged: verify the magic, recompute the content hash, check the
 author signature, and speak only what was checked -- or refuse.

 What changes is where the bytes are. `wake-verify` takes a
 `List Integer`, which for a 2.1 MB seed would be millions of list cells;
 the seed the payload just read off its own stick is a flat buffer. So
 this chapter recomputes the same three checks over memory: `sha256-buf`
 hashes the buffer in constant heap and is byte-identical to `sha256`
 over the same bytes, and only the small fields -- the stored hash, the
 author key, the signature -- become short lists. The result is a real
 `WakeReport`, so `wake-sound` and the introduction's prose are shared
 rather than reimplemented.

 The signature is over the *stored* content hash, as `cdx-verify-signature`
 does. Recomputing the hash and comparing it to the stored one is the
 separate check; a machine that signed a hash of bytes it no longer
 carries must fail `hash-ok`, not quietly pass on a valid signature.

 We say:

Section: Header Fields

  wk-magic-ok : Integer -> Boolean
  wk-magic-ok (buf) =
    peek-byte buf 0 == 67 & peek-byte buf 1 == 68 & peek-byte buf 2 == 88 & peek-byte buf 3 == 49

 The content runs from the end of the fixed header to the far edge of the
 four sections the header describes: capabilities, effects, text, rodata.
 Each is an (offset, size) pair of little-endian 64-bit fields.

  wk-content-end : Integer -> Integer
  wk-content-end (buf) =
    let cap-end = peek-qword buf 136 + peek-qword buf 144
    in let eff-end = peek-qword buf 152 + peek-qword buf 160
    in let text-end = peek-qword buf 168 + peek-qword buf 176
    in let ro-end = peek-qword buf 184 + peek-qword buf 192
    in let e1 = if cap-end > eff-end then cap-end else eff-end
    in let e2 = if text-end > ro-end then text-end else ro-end
    in if e1 > e2 then e1 else e2

  wk-slice : Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  wk-slice (buf) (i) (stop) (acc) =
    if i >= stop then acc
    else wk-slice buf (i + 1) stop (list-push acc (peek-byte buf i))

Section: The Ceremony

  wk-bad-report : Integer -> WakeReport
  wk-bad-report (size) = WakeReport {
    size = size, magic-ok = False, hash-ok = False, signature-ok = False,
    hash-hex = "", author-hex = ""
  }

 A content end beyond the bytes actually read is a malformed header, not
 an invitation to hash memory we do not own.

  wk-verify : Integer, Integer -> WakeReport
  wk-verify (buf) (size) =
    if size < cdx-header-fixed-size then wk-bad-report size
    else if wk-magic-ok buf == False then wk-bad-report size
    else let content-end = wk-content-end buf
    in if content-end > size then wk-bad-report size
    else if content-end <= cdx-header-fixed-size then wk-bad-report size
    else let stored = wk-slice buf 8 40 []
    in let computed = hash-words-to-bytes (sha256-buf buf cdx-header-fixed-size (content-end - cdx-header-fixed-size))
    in let pub-key = wk-slice buf 40 72 []
    in let sig = wk-slice buf 72 136 []
    in WakeReport {
      size = size,
      magic-ok = True,
      hash-ok = bytes-equal stored computed 0 32,
      signature-ok = ed25519-verify pub-key stored sig,
      hash-hex = bytes-to-hex-range stored 0 32,
      author-hex = bytes-to-hex-range pub-key 0 32
    }

  wk-ceremony : Integer, Integer -> Text
  wk-ceremony (buf) (size) = wake-introduction (wk-verify buf size)
